export const lsConcepts001100: any[] = [
  { id: "ls-nama-001", title: "Śrīmātā", category: "Emergence", summary: "She who is the auspicious Mother. The syllable Śrī marks a motherhood higher than any human care, for human mothers comfort their children yet cannot lift the suffering each life must undergo, while She removes the sorrows of all beings. Doctrinally She is creator, sustainer, and dissolver at once: the cosmos issues from Her, moves by Her direction, and merges back into Her, and only Her worship ferries seekers across the ocean of repeated birth, whose current runs from sense organs through mind into craving and bondage. She is also hailed as mother of Lakṣmī, Sarasvatī, and Rudrāṇī, and the great seer Durvāsā surrenders to Her begging release from future births. In practice, place one grief before Her each morning as a child would, trusting the supreme Mother to carry what human arms cannot. (Stotra verse 1.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-001"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-002", title: "Śrīmahārājñī", category: "Emergence", summary: "She who is the Empress of the Universe. The name joins Śrī with great queen, and its secret weight lies in the hidden sixteenth syllable of the sixteen-lettered mantra, formed from the opening sounds of this very name as the Lakṣmī seed of sustenance, to be received only from a teacher. Where the first name spoke of Her creating motherhood, this one speaks of sovereign maintenance: as Mother She brings forth, as Empress She upholds the worlds, and the fuller fifteen-lettered mantra grows into sixteen as the moon grows from digits to fullness. Steady recitation of that complete mantra in prescribed count is said to end rebirth. In contemplation, honor Her ordering hand in the laws that feed and shelter you, and obey one such law gladly today. (Stotra verse 1.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-001"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-003", title: "Śrīmatsiṃhāsaneśvarī", category: "Emergence", summary: "She who is the queen of the most glorious throne. She rides the lion, king of beasts, because the lion's name shares its root with destruction, and this name unveils Her third cosmic act after creating and sustaining. The same auspicious syllable that opens the first three names also promises Lakṣmī's prosperity to Her worshippers, so reverence here bears both spiritual and material fruit. Old ritual books count eight lion-throne formulas guarded by twenty-four goddesses around the sacred diagram's center, and She is their overlord. Toward the wicked She is dissolution, yet true devotees are not broken but absorbed into Her in final merging. In practice, sit upright as before a throne and yield one stubborn fault to Her dissolving gaze. (Stotra verse 1.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-001"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-004", title: "Cidagnikuṇḍasambhūtā", category: "Emergence", summary: "She who was born in the fire-pit of Pure Consciousness. The compound joins awareness, fire-altar, and birth: cit is the attributeless ground, and the altar-fire is the light that eats darkness, namely nescience. This is no literal delivery from flames but the shining of pure knowing that burns the gloom of delusion from within, as the Lord's song declares that the fire of wisdom turns every deed to ash. When no balance of good or bad action remains, no further birth is compelled. The passage thus moves the reader from cosmic acts to inward realization. In contemplation, picture a clear flame at the heart's center and feed it one cherished ignorance, watching the light grow steadier as the fuel of delusion fails. (Stotra verse 1.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-001"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-005", title: "Devakāryasamudyatā", category: "Emergence", summary: "She who is intent on fulfilling the wishes of the gods. She rises to aid the shining ones in their war with the demons, yet inwardly the shining ones are branches of wisdom and the demons are ignorance, so Her help goes to every seeker of true knowing. The name discloses Her radiant aspect while quietly implying its twin: the still self-revealing ground and its dynamic self-awareness, through which the formless executes the world's process. An ancient dialogue gives Her two shapes, a limbed form for simple worship and a beginningless, quality-free form as the Absolute under many names. In practice, examine which side of your own inner war you fed today, and ask Her to strengthen the forces of clarity before nightfall. (Stotra verse 1.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-001"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-006", title: "Udyadbhānusahasrābhā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who has the radiance of a thousand rising suns. Rising, sun, thousand, and light combine into a dawn multiplied beyond measure, and Her hue is the red of sunrise, the color every old manual ascribes to Her. This unveils Her manifest splendor beside the subtler radiance already shown: one form woven of mantra-syllables with the sixteen-lettered hymn at its crown, one physical form now beginning its portrayal, and one supreme form met only in silent worship, all robed in red so that contemplation has a color to hold. Red also signals maternal care, the watchfulness with which She tends devotees. In practice, face the morning light briefly and imagine each ray as one thread of Her attention resting upon you. (Stotra verse 2.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-002"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-007", title: "Caturbāhusamanvitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is four-armed. With this brief name the portrayal of Her sacred body opens, and the four arms are Her four ministers through whom She governs, each to be introduced across the four names that follow. The image teaches that cosmic administration flows through distinct powers of will, wisdom, and action gathered in one sovereign person. Nothing here is mere decoration; every limb is an office. In contemplation, picture the four arms as strength surrounding you on every side, and assign each of today's four chief duties to one of them, performing the work as service under Her direction. (Stotra verse 2.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-002"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-008", title: "Rāgasvarūpapāśāḍhyā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is holding the rope of love in Her hand. Craving itself becomes the noose in Her lower left hand, and with it She draws the longings of devotees toward Herself so they never drown in wanting. This is the power of sacred desire, first of the three potencies alongside wisdom and action, and it works through the horse-mounted goddess who shares this arm. A secret seed of enchantment sleeps inside the syllables of the name, built from the fire of the gracious Lord joined to longing sealed with a point. In practice, take one restless wish and deliberately offer its energy upward, letting Her rope turn a pull of appetite into a pull toward home. (Stotra verse 2.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-002"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-009", title: "Krodhākārāṅkuśojjvalā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who shines, bearing the goad of anger. The elephant-hook blazes in Her hand to pierce hatred and goad the devotee toward wisdom, for this name concerns the subtle inward frame where knowing dwells. Anger reversed becomes understanding: the sting that scattered attention is re-aimed until it prods delusion itself. Within the syllables hides the fierce seed of the dark Mother who destroys every evil, and the elephant-borne goddess of plenty wields this arm. In contemplation, recall one recent flash of irritation and visualize it cooled and straightened into a hook that drags a single ignorance into daylight. The hook that steers elephants symbolizes reason steering passion, and devotees pray for exactly such guidance in heated hours. (Stotra verse 2.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-002"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-010", title: "Manorūpekṣukodaṇḍā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who holds in Her hand a sugarcane bow that represents the mind. Mind carries both resolve and wavering, the Yes and the otherwise with which impressions from the senses ripen into thought and then into deed. Her upper left hand bends this mind into a bow of sweet cane: crush the cane and honeyed juice flows, master the mind and the sweet reality of the Absolute runs free. The minister of counsel, the dark-blue goddess of mantra-wisdom, stands behind this arm. In practice, catch the mind once today between resolve and wavering, press it gently as cane in a mill, and taste the drop of quiet that answers. (Stotra verse 3.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-003"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-011", title: "Pañcatanmātrasāyakā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who holds the five subtle elements as arrows. Where the last name gave the bow, this gives the shafts: five flower-tipped arrows for sound, touch, sight, taste, and smell, the fine essences of ether, air, fire, water, and earth, named in old manuals as lotus and four garden blooms and charged with excitement, frenzy, bewilderment, kindling, and arrest. She aims them not at enemies but at Her own devotees, to shatter the spell those very senses cast, and the boar-faced goddess bears this upper right arm. Hidden seed-sounds crowd these four weapon-names, which is why the hymn is ranked with revealed scripture and is never to be sung for display. In practice, notice one sense-arrow in flight today and let it pierce a vanity instead of a pleasure. (Stotra verse 3.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-003"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-012", title: "Nijāruṇaprabhāpūramajjadbrahmāṇḍamaṇḍalā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "she who immerses the entire universe in the red efflugence of Her form. Her rose-red splendor pours outward until the whole world-egg floats in it like a petal in dye, and with this flood the portrayal of Her gross splendor properly begins. Convention describes gods from feet to crown and goddesses from crown to feet, while the Lord who is half Her is told both ways, and the first division of Her fifteen-lettered mantra is meditated upon Her head in keeping with that order. In contemplation, picture your surroundings slowly reddening with dawn-light until persons and tasks alike glow within one compassionate medium, and act from inside that glow. (Stotra verse 3.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-003"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-013", title: "Campakāśokapunnāgasaugandhikalasatkacā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose hair has been adorned with flowers like campaka, ashoka, punnāga and saugandhika. Four fragrant blooms rest in Her tresses, yet the wonder is reversed: the flowers borrow sweetness from Her hair, which is ever perfumed of itself. A hymn of beauty sings that blossoms of heaven's gardens lodge there to win their scent, and the seer Durvāsā meditates on that hair within the heart's lotus. Moisture in the image speaks compassion, softness speaks motherhood, and the four blooms veil the fourfold inner instrument of mind, intellect, awareness, and ego. If even Her hair banishes nescience, what must Her full presence accomplish. In practice, breathe in slowly and imagine ignorance loosening like a knot in dark hair. (Stotra verse 4.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-004"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-014", title: "Kuruvindamaṇiśreṇīkanatkoṭīramaṇḍitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is resplendent with a crown adorned with rows of kuruvinda gems. The rare red ruby of Her diadem is prized as a kindler of love, fortune, and devotion toward Her own brother, the sustaining Lord. To meditate on that crimson crown is to invite both inward ripening and outward well-being. A hymn of beauty marvels that its gold set with twelve suns makes the crescent caught in its gems look like a rainbow, and the seer's great hymn likewise lingers on this crown. In contemplation, set a point of red light above your crown and feel it warm thought into affection and affection into steady worship. (Stotra verse 4.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-004"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-015", title: "Aṣṭamīcandravibhrājadalikasthalaśobhitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose forehead shines like the crescent moon of the eighth night of the lunar half-month. On the eighth day from new or full, the moon shows two even curves of perfect symmetry, neither waxing pride nor waning loss, and such is Her brow. The image commends balance: awareness poised exactly between growth and release, luminous without excess. In contemplation, picture that half-moon of cool light upon your own forehead whenever judgment runs to extremes, and let its level glow restore measure to thought and speech. The even half-moon also figures the balance of waxing devotion and waning ego, neither clinging to growth nor grieving any release. (Stotra verse 5.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-005"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-016", title: "Mukhacandrakalaṅkābhamṛganābhiviśeṣakā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who wears a musk mark on Her forehead which shines like the spot in the moon. A dark-gold paste of musk rests where the moon bears its familiar blemish, turning supposed flaw into ornament, as the moon's spot completes rather than spoils its face. The seer's hymn takes Her whole countenance as its meditation at this point. In practice, stop hiding one small imperfection today and wear it as She wears the mark, a dark point that lets surrounding brightness shine by contrast. Darkness set within brightness perfects the whole, teaching that acknowledged faults become the foil of sanctity rather than its ruin. (Stotra verse 5.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-005"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-017", title: "Vadanasmaramāṅgalyagṛhatoraṇacillikā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose eyebrows shine like the archways leading to the house of kāma, the god of love, which Her face resembles. Her face is the auspicious palace of the love-god, and Her brows are the festooned arches at its gate, for legend says he built his mansion copying Her features. Desire finds its archetype and its limit in Her: love is holy when it dwells in that house and strays when it leaves it. In contemplation, frame every affection with those twin arches, asking whether the feeling would be at home in Her palace before giving it entry. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 6.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-006"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-018", title: "Vaktralakṣmīparīvāhacalanmīnābhalocanā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose eyes possess the luster of the fish that move about in the stream of beauty flowing from Her face. Her face is the pond and Her glances are bright fish darting through it, swift because grace must reach the whole cosmos without delay. As the mother fish ripens her eggs by gaze alone, so the Goddess nourishes worlds by looking, which is why She is hailed in fish-eyed titles. In practice, rest your own gaze today: look once with deliberate kindness upon someone who cannot repay you, and let that glance feed them as Hers feeds all. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 6.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-006"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-019", title: "Navacampakapuṣpābhanāsādaṇḍavirājitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is resplendent with a nose that has the beauty of a newly blossoming campaka flower. Her nose rises straight and tender as a fresh golden bud, delicacy itself given form. The single image teaches refinement of the senses: the organ of breath and scent shaped into blossom suggests perception purified until it inhales only what is sweet and true. In contemplation, breathe slowly through the imagined flower, releasing one stale resentment with each exhalation until the inner air smells clean. Breath passing through such a blossom grows subtle, and masters read the nose as the gate where the life-wind first learns refinement. (Stotra verse 7.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-007"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-020", title: "Tārākāntitiraskārināsābharaṇabhāsurā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who shines with a nose-ornament that excels the luster of a star. Her nose-jewel of ruby and pearl outshines the very stars, and the two presiding feminine star-powers are read in its twin stones, with the red and white planets lending their gems. Hence honoring Her is said to soften planetary harshness in a devotee's life. In practice, when anxieties about fate and timing gather, picture that small steady jewel outshining whole constellations, and trust the Mother's face above every chart. The jewel further promises that planetary terrors shrink before a Mother's ornament, for grace outranks every circling power. (Stotra verse 7.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-007"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-021", title: "Kadambamañjarīkḷptakarṇapūramanoharā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is captivating, wearing bunches of kadamba flowers as ear-ornaments. Petals from the groves around Her wish-gem palace rest against Her ears, or slip down from Her hair to the ear's edge, and their unearthly scent is confessed to flow from Her earlobes rather than from any garden. Hearing itself is garlanded: what enters Her ears is already praise. In practice, guard the gate of hearing today, admitting one fewer harsh word and repeating one kind sentence, so your ears grow a little like Hers. Sound offered through garlanded ears turns into remembrance, and the devotee who listens well begins to hear all speech as Her praise. (Stotra verse 8.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-008"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-022", title: "Tāṭaṅkayugalībhūtatapanoḍupamaṇḍalā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who wears the sun and the moon as a pair of large earrings. The two great lights that sustain all life hang at Her ears, declaring Her command over cosmic rhythm, and they are also read as Her eyes and as the twin curves of the love-seed syllable guarded for initiation. A hymn of beauty marvels that the deathless Lord's own immunity from age and poison flows from the greatness of these ornaments. In contemplation, feel day and night, activity and rest, as Her paired jewels swinging in balance, and let your own labor and sleep obey their rhythm. (Stotra verse 8.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-008"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-023", title: "Padmarāgaśilādarśaparibhāvikapolabhūḥ", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose cheeks excel mirrors made of rubies in their beauty. Her cheeks are soft, glowing, and mirror-bright, outdoing slabs of red ruby, and everything reddens them further: Her own rose complexion, the crimson ornaments, and the sun and moon swinging at Her ears. Lapidaries rank four ruby kinds with the priestly stone highest and warn that flawed gems wound their wearers, yet Her cheeks are flawless reflectors of compassion, which red always signifies. A hymn of beauty seats the love-god's war-chariot upon that mirrored field. In practice, polish the cheek of your own conduct until another can see kindness reflected there. (Stotra verse 9.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-009"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-024", title: "Navavidrumabimbaśrīnyakkāriradanacchadā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose lips excel freshly cut coral and bimba fruit in their reflective splendor. New coral and the ripe red gourd-fruit are the poets' measures for beautiful lips, and Her mouth outshines both in color and light. Speech framed by such lips carries the authority of beauty: what is lovely to behold prepares the hearer to receive truth. In practice, weigh one sentence today until it is ripe and red with goodwill before letting it pass your lips. Ripe redness signals readiness to nourish, and lips of such color remind every speaker that true words should feed their hearers like fruit. (Stotra verse 9.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-009"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-025", title: "Śuddhavidyāṅkurākāradvijapaṅktidvayojjvalā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who has radiant teeth which resemble the buds of pure knowledge. Her two rows gleam like twin sprouts of the stainless wisdom, the secret non-dual worship that declares identity with the Absolute. The sixteen-lettered seed-mantra unfolding into two leaves yields thirty-two, the count of teeth, of initiations, and of opening letters of this very hymn, while the joined jaws image soul and ground seemingly two yet one. That wisdom ranks as the fifteenth principle where I and This shine equally and identity threads diversity. In practice, let each word pass a gate of thirty-two guardians, speaking only what non-dual kindness can approve. (Stotra verse 10.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-010"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-026", title: "Karpūravīṭikāmodasamākarṣidigantarā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is enjoying a camphor-laden betel roll, the fragrance of which is attracting people from all directions. Her chew blends saffron, cardamom, clove, camphor, musk, nutmeg, mace, and crystallized sugar upon the leaf, and its perfume drifts to every quarter of the world. The wise reach Her by devotion, but the unripe must be lured, and this scent is the lure of grace, the same fragrance sung in the three-hundred hymn. In practice, make your own presence fragrant today with one unadvertised courtesy, drawing others toward goodness without argument. Even lifeless ingredients, crushed and mingled, learn harmony in Her service, hinting that discordant souls blend likewise in worship. (Stotra verse 10.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-010"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-027", title: "Nijasallāpamādhuryavinirbhartsitakacchapī", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who excels even the veeṇa of sarasvatī in the sweetness of Her speech. The goddess of learning plays the lute named after the tortoise, and its melody in master hands is the summit of music, yet the Mother's casual conversation puts it to shame, until the player herself, so a hymn of beauty jests, packs her instrument away. Like the betel-perfume before it, this sweetness draws the ignorant whom bare doctrine cannot move. In contemplation, speak one necessary sentence today in a tone so gentle it could be mistaken for song, and notice how resistance softens. Melody prepares the heart for meaning, and sweetness of voice opens doors that stern argument finds bolted. (Stotra verse 11.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-011"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-028", title: "Mandasmitaprabhāpūramajjatkāmeśamānasā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who submerges even the mind of Kāmeśa (Lord shiva) in the radiance of Her smile. Her specialty is the gracious half-smile, and seated upon the left thigh of the Lord of desire as His queen, She drowns even His vast mind in its flood. Desire also names the mystic point, twin-sunned and twin-mooned within the love-seed, seat of ego and cradle of wanting, so when His mind is swayed by Her smile it proves Her glory over the very root of craving. With that same smile She lures the ignorant and pours wisdom into them. In practice, answer one provocation today with a genuine small smile, and watch how it steadies both giver and receiver. (Stotra verse 11.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-011"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-029", title: "Anākalitasādṛśyacibukaśrīvirājitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose chin cannot be compared to anything (it is beyond camporison because of its unparalleled beauty). Her chin admits no simile in heaven or earth, standing alone as the finish of a face without rival. A hymn of beauty pictures the Lord lifting it with His hand, eager to drink the nectar of Her lower lip, so even supremacy turns servant before this detail. In contemplation, rest attention on one small perfect thing today, a shell, a syllable, a child's finger, and let its incomparability teach you how the Goddess finishes all Her works. The chin props the mouth from below, hinting that silent resolve must underbear every spoken vow, and that firmness completes beauty. (Stotra verse 12.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-012"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-030", title: "Kāmeśabaddhamāṅgalyasūtraśobhitakandharā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose neck is adorned with the marriage thread tied by Kāmeśa. The wedding cord knotted by the Lord Himself graces Her throat, sealing Their union before all worlds. A hymn of beauty reads the three fine lines of Her neck as the frets marking the scale where melodies dwell and the bounds where treasures are stored. Though the cord belongs to later custom unknown to the oldest hymns, seers of bodily marks count triple lines on brow, eye, or hip as seals of fortune. In practice, honor one bond you have tied, to spouse, friend, or teacher, with a small deliberate fidelity today. (Stotra verse 12.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-012"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-031", title: "Kanakāṅgadakeyūrakamanīyabhujānvitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose arms are beautifully adorned with golden armlets. Bracelets and upper-arm bands of gold circle Her limbs, differing in shape yet one in substance. The image carries the whole metaphysic in miniature: living forms vary endlessly like ornaments, while the indwelling ground stays single like the gold. In contemplation, look at three very different people today and rehearse silently that one awareness shines in all, letting the ornament teach the metal. Gold neither tarnishes nor diminishes however often reshaped, picturing a Self that lends itself to countless births and bodies without any loss, stain, or weariness. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 13.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-013"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-032", title: "Ratnagraiveyacintākalolamuktāphalānvitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose neck is resplendent with a gem-studded necklace with a locket made of pearl. A gold pendant set with gems and a swaying chain of pearls swing upon Her throat, and their trembling figures the unsteady mind. Worshippers who can hold only part of Her splendor are the swaying ones, while those who grasp the whole head-to-foot form are the freed, and each reaps the fruit matching his steadiness. In practice, choose one short meditation today and refuse every distraction for its full span, training the swaying chain into stillness. Pearls mature through irritation patiently endured, promising that steadiness under distraction ripens at last into luminous adornment. (Stotra verse 13.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-013"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-033", title: "Kāmeśvarapremaratnamaṇipratipaṇastanī", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who gives Her breasts to Kāmeśvara in return for the gem of love He bestows on Her. She meets His jewel of affection with the nourishment of Her own bosom, love answering love in full measure. The hidden promise runs to every devotee: whatever devotion is laid before Her returns doubled as blessing. In practice, give first today without calculating return, a little more patience than was asked, and observe how generosity comes back wearing Her face. Nourishment given in love multiplies mysteriously, and seers cite this bridal exchange as proof that true sacrifice never impoverishes its giver. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 14.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-014"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-034", title: "Nābhyālavālaromālilatāphalakucadvayī", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose breasts are the fruits on the creeper of the fine hairline that starts in the depths of Her navel and spreads upwards. The tender line climbing from navel to heart puts forth twin fruits above, and the verse fixes attention on the navel and heart centers: when the coiled energy rises from the lower to the upper, meditation bears fruit. A hymn of beauty adds that the love-god himself, scorched elsewhere, took shelter in that navel depth. In contemplation, draw breath slowly from navel to heart and imagine understanding ripening there like fruit on a vine. Vines climb by clinging, and devotion likewise ascends by holding fast to the Mother through every season. (Stotra verse 14.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-014"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-035", title: "Lakṣyaromalatādhāratāsamunneyamadhyamā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who has a waist, the existence of which can only be inferred by the fact that the creeper of Her hairline springs from it. Her middle is so fine it escapes sight and must be guessed from the hair-creeper it supports. The secrecy is the teaching: the Self is subtler than any sense and yields only to keen inward observation. In practice, spend a few minutes tracing one subtle sensation, the pause between breaths, until the habit of noticing the almost-invisible grows strong. Inference ranks among valid means of knowing, and this waist trains seekers to trust disciplined reasoning about the imperceptible. (Stotra verse 15.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-015"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-036", title: "Stanabhāradalanmadhyapaṭṭabandhavalitrayā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose abdomen has three folds which form a belt to support Her waist from breaking under the weight of Her breasts. A golden band steadies a middle bowed beneath fullness, creasing into three graceful lines. A hymn of beauty tells how love himself bound Her hips with triple strands to guard them, and seers read the three lines as Her triple office of creating, maintaining, and withdrawing, with compassion outweighing all, as befits the supreme Mother. In practice, review your own threefold day, what you began, sustained, and released, and ask whether kindness outweighed the rest. Belts bind garments against storms, and triple discipline girds the seeker against the winds of distraction. (Stotra verse 15.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-015"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-037", title: "Aruṇāruṇakausumbhavastrabhāsvatkaṭītaṭī", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose hips are adorned with a garment as red as the rising sun, which is dyed with an extract from safflower (kusumbha) blossoms. Safflower-dyed silk the color of dawn girds Her loins, and since all red about Her means compassion, the verse declares that Her three cosmic acts are performed in tenderness. One of the eight seer-poetesses who composed this hymn, the ruddy goddess of dawn-speech, is also heard dwelling at this waist. In practice, clothe one necessary severity today, a correction or refusal, in dawn-red gentleness, so duty and kindness move together. Safflower yields its dye only under pressure, hinting that devotion pressed by trial colors all conduct most deeply and lastingly. (Stotra verse 16.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-016"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-038", title: "Ratnakiṅkiṇikāramyaraśanādāmabhūṣitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is adorned with a girdle which is decorated with many gem-studded bells. A zone of tiny jewel-bells rings Her hips with soft music. The ornament also maps the middle division of Her fifteen-lettered mantra upon Her body: face for the first portion, face-to-hip for the middle portion of longing, hip-downward for the final portion of power, so the whole mantra hides within the portrait from the thirteenth to the forty-seventh name. In contemplation, feel each step you take today as a small bell-note, and let walking itself become quiet recitation. Bells announce movement while remaining hollow, teaching that the wise proclaim Her march without claiming solidity for themselves. (Stotra verse 16.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-016"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-039", title: "Kāmeśajñātasaubhāgyamārdavorudvayānvitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "The beauty and softness of whose thighs are known only to kāmeśa, Her husband. What is most intimate in Her form belongs to the Lord alone, and the reserve is deliberate: from this point the final mantra-portion begins, whose power-tradition guards as strictly private. Secrecy here protects sanctity rather than hiding defect. In practice, keep one practice wholly private today, an offering, a tear, a thanksgiving, unposted and unpraised, learning the dignity of the unshown. Veiling protects power from casual eyes, and initiates learn that the holiest forces must ripen in darkness before they can safely serve in light. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 17.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-017"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-040", title: "Māṇikyamakuṭākārajānudvayavirājitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose knees are like crowns shaped from the precious red jewel, mānikya (a kind of ruby). Each knee gleams as a single ruby carved into a little crown, redness answering redness down the whole descent of Her form. Royalty kneels only to love, and even Her joints wear diadems. In practice, bend the knee of pride once today, conceding a point gracefully, and discover the crown hidden inside humility. Knees bear the body's whole weight whenever the devotee kneels, so jeweled knees declare that even postures of lowliness carry royal splendor within Her kingdom, and no prostration ever shames the prostrate. (Stotra verse 17.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-017"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-041", title: "Indragopaparikṣiptasmaratūṇābhajaṅghikā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose calves gleam like the jewel-covered quiver of the God of Love. Her lower legs shine as the gem-cased arrow-holder of the love-god, ringed with tiny glowing creatures like living jewels. A hymn of beauty explains the strategy: to win the Lord's heart, love made Her limbs his arsenal, with ten toenail arrows waiting in the case. In contemplation, consider what quiver you carry into others' hearts, and resolve that your approaches bring delight rather than wounds. Quivers hold deaths yet gleam with fine craft, warning that attraction ungoverned always wounds, while beauty guided by love protects and delights. (Stotra verse 18.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-018"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-042", title: "Gūḍhagulphā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose ankles are hidden. Her ankles are round, shapely, and modestly concealed, perfection that does not display itself. With the calves and arches around it, this detail follows the science of bodily marks by which the wise read character in form. In practice, perfect one small hidden thing today, a drawer, a ledger entry, a private habit, letting unseen order mirror Her unseen ankles. Anklets ring about what the robe conceals, suggesting that hidden virtues still make music for attentive ears, and that modesty sings softly yet never falls silent before the Lord who hears every faintest note. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 18.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-018"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-043", title: "Kūrmapṛṣṭhajayiṣṇuprapadānvitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose feet have arches that rival the back of a tortoise in smooothness and beauty. Her insteps curve more finely than the tortoise's dome, yet the great teacher of non-duality flames at the comparison, protesting in his hymn of beauty that only Her toes sustain the universe, that the Lord Himself handled those soft feet with care at their wedding, and daring the hymn's poetesses for likening silk to shell. The quarrel itself proves this hymn older than his. In practice, place yourself beneath rather than above something small today, and serve it with the care the Lord showed those feet. (Stotra verse 18.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-018"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-044", title: "Nakhadīdhitisa'nchannanamajjanatamoguṇā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose toenails give out such a radiance that all the darkness of ignorance is dispelled completely from those devotees who prostrate at Her feet. When gods and demons bow, the gems of their crowns kindle no fire beside the light of Her toenails, whose rays eat the heavy gloom of inertia in every prostrate heart. She grants blessing and boons through those feet because Her four hands are already full, holding four goddesses of will and wisdom. In practice, bow low before sleep, touching the forehead to the ground in thought, and ask the light of Her smallest nail to thin one darkness in you. (Stotra verse 19.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-019"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-045", title: "Padadvayaprabhājālaparākṛtasaroruhā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose feet defeat lotus flowers in radiance. The lotus, measurer of divine eyes and feet, loses to the net of light Her twin soles cast. A hymn of beauty sings that the creator builds worlds from their dust, the preserver sustains from it, and the dissolver smears it as ash, while its dust scours inner blindness. Mystics count four mystic feet in white, red, mixed, and liberated hues resting on brow, heart, and crown under four divine regents for creating, sustaining, dissolving, and freeing. In practice, honor feet today, your own tired ones, a guest's, an elder's, remembering how much of heaven walks upon them. (Stotra verse 19.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-019"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-046", title: "Śiñjānamaṇimañjiramaṇḍitaśrīpadāmbujā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose auspicious lotus feet are adorned with gem-studded golden anklets that tinkle sweetly. Jeweled rings sing about Her lotus feet as She moves. That five whole names should labor over feet alone staggers the mind, and the poetesses intend the vertigo: if the lowest detail overwhelms description, what must the full splendor be, shining as still light, reflective awareness, and great enchantress in one. In practice, attend with full care to the humblest duty before you today, for heaven hides its weight in low places. Gold set with gems figures earth crowned with virtues, and feet so shod teach that the lowest offices deserve the richest care. (Stotra verse 20.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-020"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-047", title: "Marālīmandagamanā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose gait is as slow and gentle as that of a swan. Emerging from the pit of consciousness toward the waiting gods, She walks with the gliding measure of the royal swan, though all comparisons limp before a motion without peer. A hymn of beauty teases that Her household swans study those tripping feet, refusing to leave them, as pupils haunt a master. With this step the hidden mapping of the final mantra-portion closes. In practice, walk one short distance today at half your usual speed, feeling each footfall as a deliberate blessing upon the ground. Swans glide without rippling the water, picturing progress that disturbs neither the world nor the mind. (Stotra verse 20.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-020"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-048", title: "Mahālāvaṇyaśevadhiḥ", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is the treasure-house of beauty. She is not beautiful among others but the vault where all beauty is stored. A hymn of beauty confesses that even the profoundest thinkers strain and fail to find Her likeness, while heaven's maidens crowd for one glimpse, glad to dissolve into the Lord for it, a union austerities could hardly purchase. In contemplation, stop comparing beauties today and instead trace every lovely thing back to its treasury, thanking the keeper for each withdrawal. Vaults guard wealth against thieves and time, and this treasury image assures devotees that beauty never bankrupts but only enriches its beholders. (Stotra verse 20.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-020"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-049", title: "Sarvāruṇā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is entirely red in complexion. All is ruddy in Her, skin and robe and gem, for red is compassion made visible. A hymn of beauty calls that red mercy past all grasp, the revealed canon hails the copper-red dawn as most auspicious, and the three-hundred hymn repeats this very title. No further warrant is needed where scripture itself blushes. In practice, dye one act today in Her color: let a routine duty be performed with such warmth that its redness shows. Total redness leaves no corner uncompassionate, promising that no region of life lies outside the reach of Her tenderness. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-050", title: "Anavadyāṅgī", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She whose body is worthy of worship. Every limb is flawless and keeps the canon of bodily marks, without blemish or disproportion. She is the quality-free ground and the qualified splendor at once, and here the qualified face is shown perfect because the ground itself can never be marred. In practice, treat your own body today as a precinct: feed it cleanly, rest it duly, and refuse one degrading habit at its threshold. Seers read limbs as letters of a divine alphabet, and a flawless text proclaims its Author perfect beyond all questioning, so bodily reverence becomes a kind of scripture-study for the devout. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-051", title: "Sarvābharaṇabhūṣitā", category: "Rūpa", summary: "She who is resplendent with all types of ornaments. Every jewel-kind finds its place upon Her, forty in the old goddess chronicle and more in the manuals of diagram-worship, echoed again in the three-hundred hymn. Though some hold the bodily portrait closed here and others extend it a little further, all agree these closing names blend still light with reflective splendor in one gleaming figure. In practice, ornament yourself inwardly today with one virtue for each outer care you take, patience with dress, honesty with jewels. Ornament never burdens the worthy wearer, hinting that virtues beautify without ever weighing down the soul that carries them. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-052", title: "Śivakāmeśvarāṅkasthā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who sits in the lap of shiva, who is the conqueror of desire. Her enthronement opens here: She rests upon the left thigh of the gracious Lord in His form as ruler of longing, the still self-shining ground holding His dynamic reflection. The left side is the heart's side, for She is His heart, His love made person. Handsome, desiring, knowing, this Lord gathers every attribute of the qualified Absolute, and the verse shows motionless and moving energies wedded, which is also the world's creation. In meditation, picture the pair as one seat of calm and kindness, and settle your own restlessness into that lap. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-053", title: "Śivā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who bestows all that is auspicious. She is named with His own name because no particle of difference stands between them; She is graciousness itself and the very embodiment of welfare. As His desiring aspect She carries the one longing He can hold, the longing for beings to know themselves, and old sayings bind them as word to meaning, father and mother of all. She is the deluding power turned gracious, self-lit, quality-free, slayer of circling births, ocean of mercy from whom the discerning obtain everything. In practice, stop dividing your devotions today between rival powers, and serve the one auspicious presence in every face. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-054", title: "Svādhīnavallabhā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who keeps Her husband always under Her control. The consort belongs to Her alone because the still ground fashioned this moving energy for the express charge of the three cosmic acts, and without that union nothing can stir, as the hymn of beauty's opening declares. Neither acts singly: the ground needs the soul's frame to shape a life, and the soul needs the ground to be born at all. With this name the bodily portrait closes and the telling of Her city begins, while the boldness of such pictures is defended as poetry, as testing of the seeker, as Her own play of veiling, and as ladder by which dull minds climb from formed to formless. In practice, test yourself today: meet one alluring detail of the world without excitement, and pass upward. (Stotra verse 21.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-021"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-055", title: "Sumerumadhyaśṛṅgasthā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who sits on the middle peak of Mount Sumeru. The telling of Her dwelling opens here: amid the triple-peaked range, whose three summits draw a triangle, rises a loftier central crest where She abides. A devotee-seer salutes the three lower thrones of creator, preserver, and dissolver and worships the golden-rayed height between them. Mystically this is the sacred diagram itself, triangle enclosing the central point where She dwells with the great Lord of longing. In contemplation, gather your scattered concerns as three low hills and raise one silent peak of attention between them, enthroning Her there. Peaks gather clouds and release rivers, and this summit of being gathers all prayers and releases all blessings. (Stotra verse 22.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-022"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-056", title: "Śrīmannagaranāyikā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who is the Mistress of the most auspicious (or prosperous). She rules the resplendent city, built by heaven's architect in one account, risen as a gem-isle in the milk-ocean in another, with an inner wisdom-city ringed by twenty-five walls for the twenty-five principles. From that capital She administers creating and all its sequel. In practice, order one room or desk today as a small province of Her city, letting outer arrangement rehearse inner government. Capitals concentrate power for the sake of distant provinces, and this city's twenty-five ramparts remind seekers that every principle of nature answers obediently to one single throne. (Stotra verse 22.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-022"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-057", title: "Cintāmaṇigṛhāntasthā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who resides in a house built of the cintāmaṇi. Her palace in the city's northern quarter is raised from the wish-fulfilling gem that grants whatever is asked. Gods and goddesses throng there, all potent formulas trace their birth to it, and worship of it scours mental affliction. In practice, carry one legitimate longing into quiet today and lay it within that gem-house, asking only what would still shine if granted. Gems that grant wishes also test their askers, and the palace admits only those longings that would still bless their owners once fulfilled, while fevered cravings dissolve unanswered at its gem-studded gates. (Stotra verse 22.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-022"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-058", title: "Pañcabrahmāsanasthitā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who sits on a seat made of five Brahmās. Her throne stands upon the creator, the preserver, the dissolver, the veiling lord, and the ever-gracious, four as legs and one as seat, as the hymn of beauty likewise depicts, proclaiming Her supreme authority. Inwardly the five are the elements earth through ether stacked as five bodily centers from root to throat, so She sits upon the whole fabric of manifestation, to be followed past the brow's command post to crown-union. Later names return to this throne. In contemplation, feel earth, water, fire, air, and space as the chair beneath awareness, and sit lightly upon them all. (Stotra verse 22.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-022"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-059", title: "Mahāpadmāṭavīsaṃsthā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who resides in the great lotus forest. She dwells among vast lotus waters, nature's bounty after the high peaks, while lotus also names the great elephant, strength amid sweetness. Inwardly this is the thousand-petalled crown, whose minute central aperture alone admits divine energy and joins all lower centers, where She meets the Lord. In practice, picture a lotus opening at your crown today and let one stiff opinion soften petal by petal. Forests shelter countless lives without reckoning or partiality, picturing a crown-center spacious enough to host every god and every wandering thought together in one shade. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 23.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-023"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-060", title: "Kadambavanavāsinī", category: "Abode", summary: "She who resides in the kadamba forest. A grove of divinely fragrant kadamba rings Her gem-house between the silver and golden ramparts, proclaiming Her earth-principle and motherhood of soil. There the diagram's goddesses cross and meet, the six seasons with their regents keep palaces, and the dark-blue minister keeps her court as mistress of ninety wisdom-seeds. No name here is idle scenery; each hides a seed-sound for the ripe. In practice, walk among trees or plants today and greet them as wards of Her grove. Groves breathe coolness upon travelers, and this fragrant ring promises rest to pilgrims scorched by the deserts of argument. (Stotra verse 23.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-023"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-061", title: "Sudhāsāgaramadhyasthā", category: "Abode", summary: "She who resides in the center of the ocean of nectar. She stands mid-sea in the lunar current, which within the seeker is the moon-center below the crown: when the coiled energy climbs and its heat melts that center, nectar-liquor slides down the throat and spreads through seventy-two thousand channels, the deathless drink of long-lived seers. The same sea names the diagram's central point hymned by the teacher. In contemplation, on waking imagine one cool drop of sweetness descending from the crown, and let the body drink calm before words begin. Seas receive all rivers without rising, picturing a heart-center that absorbs every experience yet never overflows its peace. (Stotra verse 23.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-023"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-062", title: "Kāmākṣī", category: "Abode", summary: "She whose eyes awaken desire, or She who has beautiful eyes. Her gaze is lovely because grace, pity, and mercy fill it, and by looks alone She ripens devotees' longings. Mystic grammar finds two goddesses, speech and fortune, enthroned as those eyes, while another reading makes Her the very eyes of the Lord of longing. In practice, meet one gaze today with unhurried kindness, letting your eyes learn a little of Hers. Glances accomplish what armies cannot, and one look of pure goodwill often disarms hearts where long persuasion fails entirely, for sight is the swiftest messenger of the soul. (Stotra verse 23.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-023"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-063", title: "Kāmadāyinī", category: "Abode", summary: "She who grants all wishes. She gives whatever is sought, and most of all She gives the Lord of longing Himself, for none reaches the still ground except through Her: She is the veil around Him that only Her own will lifts. The creator honored Her omniscience with the two love-titles, won by works performed with a glance, and later readings hide four attendant goddesses within the four grove-names. With this the gross portrait closes and the supreme deeds begin. In practice, examine one wish today to see whether it leads toward the ground or away, and offer only the former. (Stotra verse 23.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-023"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-064", title: "Devarṣigaṇasaṅghātastūyamānātmavaibhavā", category: "Abode", summary: "She whose might is the subject of praise by multitudes of gods and sages. Shining ones, seers, and sevenfold hosts of attendants celebrate Her all-pervading majesty, and such worshippers bow to none below the Absolute, which quietly proves Her rank. Legend adds that the wandering seer begged Her to destroy the great demon plaguing those very hosts, while inwardly the demons are ego born of nescience and the prayer is for absolution from selfhood, since sin springs from self. In practice, join that choir briefly today: praise Her aloud once, then silently, and feel ego thin between the two singings. (Stotra verse 24.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-024"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-065", title: "Bhaṇḍāsuravadhodyuktaśaktisenāsamanvitā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who is endowed with an army of shaktis intent on slaying bhaṇḍāsura. She marshals the diagram's goddesses for war with the demon born when the elephant-lord playfully shaped the love-god's ashes and the creator applauded the deed. His name also means bondage, which an aphorism defines as cramped knowing, the primal littleness that shrinks unbounded awareness into a separate soul. By fierce collected attention Her energies erase that illusory world and leave supreme consciousness, which is liberation offered to all who turn toward Her. In practice, muster your own small army today, one habit, one prayer, one restraint, against one entrenched ignorance. (Stotra verse 24.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-024"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-066", title: "Sampatkarīsamārūḍhasindhuravrajasevitā", category: "Battle", summary: "Who is attended by a herd of elephants ably commanded by sampatkarī. Elephant files led by the goddess of plenty serve Her, she who was met earlier as the goad-arm and mistress of a potent three-lettered lore built from joy, fire, and comfort sealed with a point. Ceaseless repetition of that seed, keyed by a teacher's living syllable, grants full prosperity, while knowers read a deeper triad of knower, known, and knowing fused into blissful fortune. Single-pointed fusion with Her energy, the aphorisms say, even shapes the body to its will. In contemplation, steady one wobbling resolve today until it stands massed and calm as elephants in line. (Stotra verse 25.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-025"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-067", title: "Aśvārūḍhādhiṣṭhitāśvakoṭikoṭibhirāvṛtā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who is surrounded by a cavalry of several million horses which are under the command of the shakti, aśvārūḍhā. Countless squadrons wheel about Her under the horse-faced goddess of the noose-arm, whose thirteen-seeded formula, caged front and back, ripens swiftly. Horses are the senses, fast and headlong when loose, and mind alone rides them; an aphorism calls the stages of ascent a wondrous marvel as awareness climbs station by station to the sleepless fourth where bliss dawns. The old chariot image agrees: body for carriage, senses for horses, road for objects, driver for discrimination. In practice, rein one sense-horse today, refusing it one gallop, and feel the rider grow stronger. (Stotra verse 25.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-025"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-068", title: "Cakrarājarathārūḍhasarvāyudhapariṣkṛtā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who shines in Her chariot cakrarāja, equipped with all kinds of weapons. Her nine-tiered war-car gleams with every arm, and its weapons are pathways to stainless wisdom. The car is the sacred diagram of nine enclosures, five downward triangles of energy and four upward of stillness, housing forty-three goddesses and seventy-nine attendants where every divinity may be adored, confirmed by a later name of this hymn. The same car within is the six bodily centers as foundation for mastery through pure knowing, whereby the adept becomes one with the still Lord. In practice, sketch or study the diagram briefly today, then close the eyes and find its courts within. (Stotra verse 26.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-026"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-069", title: "Geyacakrarathārūḍhamantriṇīparisevitā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who is served by the shakti named mantriṇī who rides the chariot known as geyacakra. Flanking the sovereign's car runs the tuneful car of the minister, the dark-blue counselor of the bow-arm, who worships rather than merely obeys. Her seventy-nine attendants are masters of the secret lore, and the important car is also the diagram itself, where meditators win formula-power swiftly and become counselors in turn. The body mirrors those nine enclosures from crown-orifice to feet, grounding excess heaven through the soles, while an aphorism reveals formulas as the Mother's own letters of light. In practice, treat your body as diagram today, keeping each gate clean for Her passage. (Stotra verse 26.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-026"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-070", title: "Kiricakrarathārūḍhadaṇḍanāthāpuraskṛtā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who is escorted by the shakti known as daṇḍanāthā, seated in the kirichakra chariot. On the other flank rides the staff-bearer, the boar-faced general of the arrow-arm, in a car shaped like her visage. Boar also means rays, and light is creation's dawn, as every genesis-tale opens with brightness; her wheel is the round of making, keeping, and dissolving through which the rider passes fearless, knowing the body perishes but the Self abides. Pleasure and pain touch only the outer instruments, and freed attention merges with supreme awareness at the brow's station. In practice, endure one small discomfort today without complaint, rehearsing the general's indifference to the perishable. (Stotra verse 27.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-027"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-071", title: "Jvālāmālinikākṣiptavahniprākāramadhyagā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who has taken position at the center of the fortress of fire created by the goddess, jvālāmālinī. The goddess of the fourteenth lunar tide raised a rampart of flame at Her bidding, and mystically that wall is the diagram's five energy-triangles mingled with four stillness-triangles, with the Mother dwelling at their middle point. The wise wear this garland of fire that eats nescience: like sparks that flare, fade to ash, and are merely watched by the flame, deeds perish while witnessing awareness abides, the dynamic teaching of tremor and aphorism. She performs the three acts yet partakes in none. In practice, do one duty today as pure witness, giving full care to the deed while refusing its fever. (Stotra verse 27.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-027"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-072", title: "Bhaṇḍasainyavadhodyuktaśaktivikramaharṣitā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who rejoices at the valor of the shaktis who are intent on destroying the forces of bhaṇḍāsura. She delights as Her energies crush the demon's host, for the dull one's name is the ignorant soul of duality, his army is the sense of otherness, and their slaying is the death of division. Her joy breaks out whenever the veil of seeming tears through inward inquiry. In practice, give Her one victory to celebrate today: reconcile one us-against-them thought into a single household of being. Generals celebrate every skirmish won, teaching devotees to mark small conquests over habit with gratitude instead of postponing all joy to final victory. (Stotra verse 28.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-028"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-073", title: "Nityāparākramāṭopanirīkṣaṇasamutsukā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who delights in seeing the might and the pride of Her nityā deities. She watches eagerly as the fifteen lunar-tide goddesses prove their mettle in the fray. Once duality falls and the veil lifts, wisdom of the ground keeps ripening by stages, and ground once gained is not lightly lost. In practice, review one spiritual gain of this past year and guard it this week with the jealousy of a general inspecting troops. Seedlings need fences far more than grown forests do, and young spiritual attainments likewise deserve vigilant guarding until they stand rooted beyond any reversal. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 28.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-028"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-074", title: "Bhaṇḍaputravadhodyuktabālāvikramananditā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who delights in seeing the valor of the goddess bāla who is intent on killing the sons of bhaṇḍa. Her nine-year-old daughter begged leave for battle and destroyed the demon's thirty sons against a mother's caution. That maiden's formula is every beginner's first initiation, and mastery of it is said to wake the hidden virtues of healing herbs; she is the sovereign's own limb-goddess among limb, branch, and sub-branch divinities. Inwardly the thirty slain are the thirty principles that must be crossed before release, and a little sincere effort lays them low. In practice, attempt one small discipline today beyond your years, trusting littleness in Her service to slay giants. (Stotra verse 29.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-029"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-075", title: "Mantriṇyambāviracitaviṣaṅgavadhatoṣitā", category: "Battle", summary: "(viśukravadhatoṣitā) (See a note below) She who rejoices at the destruction, in battle, of the demon viṣanga by the mantriṇī shakti. She exults as the motherly minister, the dark-blue counselor, slays the shoulder-born brother of the dull demon. A seed-sound vee sleeps in the name, rooted in the watery letter that both grants wondrous powers and scours evil influence, and the minister herself is the potency of sacred formulas. Her victim embodies cravings bred by the senses' misuse, which formula-power in a devotee destroys. In practice, repeat one chosen sacred phrase with attention today whenever a sense-craving rises, letting the minister fight beside you. (Stotra verse 29.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-029"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-076", title: "Viśukraprāṇaharaṇavārāhīvīryananditā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who is pleased with the prowess of vārāhī who took the life of vishukra. She applauds as the boar-faced general takes the second shoulder-born brother's life-breath. With the maiden and the minister, this third warrior cleanses the stains the senses leave: the maiden lends bodily strength to receive power descending through crown and hinder head, the minister lends the mastery of formulas counted to completion with their rites, and the general lends the austerity that brooks no slackness in sense or conduct. Together strength, sound, and discipline ripen the soul to wear the body as a mere sheath toward merging. In practice, add one small austerity today, earlier rising or plainer food, and offer its edge to Her general. (Stotra verse 30.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-030"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-077", title: "Kāmeśvaramukhālokakalpitaśrīgaṇeśvarā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who gives rise to gaṇeśa by a glance at the face of kāmeśvara. From one look exchanged with the Lord of longing, the elephant-lord was projected to meet a crisis: the foe had planted a victory-charm of obstacles amid Her ranks, sapping their confidence, and only a conqueror of the twenty-sevenfold citadel of senses, instruments, breaths, elements, inner faculties, craving, nescience, and deeds, crowned by the Lord's own quality, could uproot it. His twenty-eightfold root-formula mirrors that citadel plus grace, and an aphorism promises that for whom the elements are mere coverings, liberation is already present and godlike. In practice, glance at the Lord's face, in image or inwardly, before tackling one blocked task today. (Stotra verse 30.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-030"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-078", title: "Mahāgaṇeśanirbhinnavighnayantrapraharṣitā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who rejoices when gaṇeśa shatters all obstacles. She exults as the great remover smashes the foe's implanted charm and Her army breathes confidence again. All obstruction-works are figures of seeming, and seeming is Her own projection, which She alone can lift, yet She lifts it in measure of striving, which is why a later name hails Her as teacher. In practice, persist one more round against a stubborn block today instead of abandoning the field, and trust the elephant-lord's tread behind your effort. Blocked armies need engineers before heroes, and the remover-god clears roads so that subtler powers can afterward march unhindered. (Stotra verse 31.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-031"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-079", title: "Bhaṇḍāsurendranirmuktaśastrapratyastravarṣiṇī", category: "Battle", summary: "She who showers counter weapons to each weapon fired at Her by bhaṇḍāsura. Every missile loosed by the dull lord, whether hurled from afar like a shell or gripped in hand like a blade, She answers with its antidote in raining plenty. Her armory serves our own ascent: the darts leaving Her hands aim at the illusion of twoness in us. With the two elephant-lord names these steps form a ladder of awakening and Her aid upon it. In practice, meet one hostile force today, a temper, a rumor, a temptation, with its precise opposite, and watch it burst like an intercepted shell. (Stotra verse 31.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-031"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-080", title: "Karāṅgulinakhotpannanārāyaṇadaśākṛtiḥ", category: "Battle", summary: "She who created from Her fingernails all ten incarnations of Ṇārāyaṇa (viṣṇu). When the foe conjured ten fiends from his all-demon missile, She effortlessly grew ten saviors from Her fingertips. Ten here also counts the five stations of mortals, waking, dreaming, deep sleep, the witnessing fourth, and the non-dual beyond, together with the five offices of the ground, creating through merging, for the name means both the individual soul and its Lord. Since the preserving Lord is Her own brother, seers read the mystic reading above the mythic one, and nail-birth itself confesses how playfully She makes. In practice, rehearse the witness today: note waking, dreaming, and sleeping as they pass, and rest as the knower of all three. (Stotra verse 32.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-032"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-081", title: "Mahāpāśupatāstrāgninirdagdhāsurasainikā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who burned the armies of the demons in the fire of the missile, mahāpāśupata. She reduced the whole demon camp with the great lord-of-beasts weapon whose flame spares nothing of duality. The old chronicle of the phallic emblem praises that observance as divine release from bondage, and its six-syllabled formula joins the primal hum of making, keeping, and dissolving to the five syllables of homage, addressed to the ever-gracious face of the five-visaged Lord. All such arms mean the mind's march from twoness to oneness through drill. In practice, repeat the five-syllabled homage slowly today, letting each round burn a little more of the sense of separation. (Stotra verse 32.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-032"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-082", title: "Kāmeśvarāstranirdagdhasabhaṇḍāsuraśūnyakā", category: "Battle", summary: "She who burned and destroyed bhaṇḍāsura and his capital śūnyaka with the kāmeśvara missle. With the Lord-of-longing's own dart She fired the dull one together with his void-named capital, closing the war. This higher weapon outranks even the beast-lord's, for its namesake is the Absolute in gracious form, alike the beloved of all and the lover of all, subject and object in one, pointing toward the qualified ground at its summit. Ego with its subtle soldiery consumed, only void remains, the emptiness of ended twoness, and renunciation opens the road beyond, as the great forest-teaching bids hear, ponder, and dwell upon the Self. In practice, burn one little capital today, an archive of grudges, and enjoy the empty ground. (Stotra verse 33.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-033"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-083", title: "Brahmopendramahendrādidevasaṃstutavaibhavā", category: "Battle", summary: "She whose many powers are extolled by brahmā, viṣṇu, shiva and other gods. Victorious, She is hymned by the creator, the dwarf-striding preserver, the great Indra-form, and all the shining ones, for all acknowledge the supreme power. Praise also means inwardness, and all-pervading glory means She is met within through mind and without through sense alike, displaying the kinetic splendor of the ground. In practice, praise Her once aloud and once in silence today, honoring the outer and inner omnipresence together. Victors praised by former rivals prove their supremacy complete, and heaven's unanimous chorus of creator, preserver, and storm-lords leaves no doubter of Her throne. (Stotra verse 33.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-033"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-084", title: "Haranetrāgnisandagdhakāmasañjīvanauṣadhiḥ", category: "Battle", summary: "She who became the life-giving medicine for kāmadeva (the god of love) who had been burned to ashes by the fire from shiva's (third) eye. When the remover's brow-flame reduced love to ash, She became the resurrection-herb that raised him, a mother's rescue where a strict father's discipline had struck, for the parents' own child stood between them. Hence She wears the teacher's mantle, able to save where even sternness condemns, though the highest teacher remains the remover himself. Inwardly the burning is nescience destroyed and the rising is wisdom born, which is why Her worship is called the lore of wisdom, and the brow's eye marks the command-wheel where currents meet for final merging. In practice, revive one scorched affection today with a cool word of forgiveness. (Stotra verse 34.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-034"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-085", title: "Śrīmadvāgbhavakūṭaikasvarūpamukhapaṅkajā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She whose lotus face is the auspicious vāgbhavakūṭa (a group of syllables of the panchadashi mantra). With this name the exposition of Her fifteen-lettered formula begins and the telling of Her subtle frame opens. That frame is threefold: the subtle body of formula-sound, the subtler body of love's mystic diagram, and the subtlest body of coiled energy, and here Her lotus face is equated with the first formula-portion that grants speech and wisdom. The honorific marks reverence for the whole potent phrase. In practice, recite a wisdom-verse slowly before a mirror today, watching the face that utters it grow a little more lotus-like. (Stotra verse 34.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-034"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-086", title: "Kaṇṭhādhaḥkaṭiparyantamadhyakūṭasvarūpiṇī", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who from Her neck to Her waist is of the form of the madhyakūṭa (the middle six syllables of the pancadaśākṣari mantra). Her torso between throat and hip embodies the middle six of the fifteen, following wisdom with sacred longing and preceding it toward power, a three-step dance of knowing, willing, and doing across three names. In contemplation, breathe attention slowly from throat to navel today, feeling each breath syllable the middle of your own unspoken hymn. Torsos carry breath and digestion, the twin engines of living, hinting that middle formulas sustain the seeker's inner economy through every day and night. (Stotra verse 35.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-035"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-087", title: "Śaktikūṭaikatāpannakaṭyadhobhāgadhāriṇī", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She whose form below the waist is the śaktikūṭa (the last four syllables of the pancadaśākṣari mantra). All beneath the hip is the final fourfold power-portion, completing the mantra-body from brow to foot. What is lowest in space is last in utterance and ripest in force, teaching that foundations carry power. In practice, honor the lowly supports of your life today, feet, servants, routine tasks, as bearers of Her final syllables. Foundations lie buried yet carry whole palaces, teaching that final powers work unseen beneath all visible splendor, and that humility underbears exaltation as the hips underbear the torso. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 35.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-035"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-088", title: "Mūlamantrātmikā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who is the embodiment of the mūla mantra (the pancadaśākṣari mantra). Root of mantra means the fifteen-lettered root itself, and She is its living soul, that root being in turn the root of every other formula. Her love-diagram is the root of even that, and the whole potent phrase is laid like a second skin upon Her limbs. In practice, return today to one root phrase you trust, repeating it until sound and self feel continuous. Roots hide in darkness yet decide the whole tree, reminding seekers that silent repetition determines all the outer foliage of conduct, fortune, and final release. (Stotra verse 36.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-036"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-089", title: "Mūlakūṭatrayakalevarā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She whose (subtle) body is made of the three parts of the pancadaśākṣari mantra. Her finer frame is woven of all three mantra-portions at once, and behind them stands the love-diagram where the two great breath-syllables meet in triangle and triple point. That diagram's seed, guarded for a teacher's lips, is said to shower prosperity when rightly joined to the sixteen-lettered fullness. With this the subtle and subtler tellings close and the coiled-energy telling waits. In practice, honor triads today, beginning, middle, end in every work, and finish what you start. Weavers combine threads of three colors into one cloth, picturing triple formulas interlaced as a single seamless garment of sound. (Stotra verse 36.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-036"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-090", title: "Kulāmṛtaikarasikā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who is especially fond of the nectar known as kula. From here to the hundred-and-eleventh name Her subtlest coiled form is sung, distinct from the formula-body and diagram-body already shown, with fuller bodily details reserved for later chapters. She relishes the lineage-nectar, the ambrosial drops that fall when the coiled one reaches the crown and meets the Lord. She loves it less for taste than for union, never willing to part from Him, and the path itself from root to crown bears the same lineage-name, as does the knower-known-knowing triad whose fusion ends twoness. In practice, savor one moment of wordless peace today as a drop of that nectar, and thank the union it foretastes. (Stotra verse 36.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-036"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-091", title: "Kulasaṅketapālinī", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who protects the code of rituals of the path of yoga known as kula. Kinsfolk here are Her own worshippers, and She herself guards their signal-lore: formulas, diagram, coiled ascent, and diagram-worship, with the diagram and fifteen-lettered phrase most secret of all. Secrecy shields society from potent sounds in wrong hands and veils intimacy that public speech would profane, yet the worthy must still be taught, so this telling opens only what is needful. She withholds the rest from the unripe by Her own will. In practice, keep one confidence scrupulously today, proving yourself worthy of deeper trusts. The verse thus turns ornament into instruction, inviting slow rereading until praise ripens into practice. (Stotra verse 36.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-036"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-092", title: "Kulāṅganā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who is well-born (who is from a good family). She descends from the house of the chaste, who guard both birth-line and marriage-line honor and seldom show themselves in public, for She too hides behind the veil of seeming. In the crown She secretly meets Her consort after breaking six planes and five-and-twenty principles, a union the teacher counts as its own principle of identity. Her diagram-worship is likewise most secret, and pomp, novelty, or sloppy rite forfeits its fruit entirely. In practice, perform one rite exactly as prescribed today, neither decorating nor shortening it, and let precision be your chastity. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-093", title: "Kulāntasthā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who resides in the kula vidyā. Lineage also means the scriptures, and She dwells amid them as the goddess of letters who mothers every text. The same word names the spinal highway of ascent, and in the knower-triad She is the worthy Known, power omnipresent and, as an old forest-teaching says, apprehended at every level of the seeker's awareness. In practice, dwell ten minutes today inside one sacred sentence, turning it over until it opens rooms within rooms. Libraries house silent teachers patient as stone, and dwelling among scriptures lets long-dead sages tutor the living reader across unbridged centuries. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-094", title: "Kaulinī", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who belongs to the kula. She is the heart of the lineage's ecstatic discipline, worshipped at its center as power while the still pole stands as the non-lineage beyond. Their wedding at the crown is the lineage's own meaning, and a second thousand-petalled court below the crown keeps its own lineage-mistress with attendant powers. One of this hymn's eight poetesses bears the same name, and outer diagram-worship carries it too. In practice, honor the mirror-triad today, yourself, your act, and its object, remembering all three are Hers. Centers hold wheels together, and She at the lineage-hub keeps every revolving practice from flying apart into novelty. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-095", title: "Kulayoginī", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who is the deity in the kulas. Lineage-worship here means inward offering across the six wheels, performable only through discipline of union. The root-center is the lineage-pole and the crown beyond the wheels is the non-lineage-pole, and only yoked practice bridges them, hence her title. In practice, join breath to attention for a counted series today, letting each round be one stitch binding root to crown. Bridges join banks that could never meet otherwise, and yoked practice alone spans the gulf between root-dwelling and crown-freedom, so the goddess of the bridge deserves daily honor from every traveler on the road. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-096", title: "Akulā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who does not have a family. She claims no genealogy, being projected by the still Lord Himself, and no lineage can contain what lies past the six wheels. The central channel blooms a thousand-petalled lotus at its crown beyond all wheels, Her non-lineage seat, matched below by a two-petalled lotus distinct from the four-petalled root. Thus one word plays seven tunes from ambrosial taste to transcendence across seven names. In practice, release one label of birth or party today and rest briefly as nobody's, finding Her there. Orphans belong to all and none, and the parentless Goddess adopts every seeker whom birth has left unsheltered. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-097", title: "Samayāntasthā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who resides inside 'samaya'. She stands at the center of the inward doctrine, for lineage-names the outer rite while sameness-names the inner worship, which five ancient sages established as the stronger road. Grammar itself weds them, masculine for Him and feminine for Her, equal in five bonds: shared seat in diagram-point and crown, shared parenthood of creating, shared dance of bliss, shared titles from dread to lovely forms, and shared crimson glow where His crystal takes Her color. Never sunder the pair in altar or mind. In practice, join stillness to energy deliberately today: sit quietly, then act kindly, wedding both halves of worship. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-098", title: "Samayācāratatparā", category: "Mantra-kūṭa", summary: "She who is attached to the samaya form of worship. She loves the inward ascent taught in the old mountain-dialogue: led wheel by wheel from the root by a teacher, bathed in initiation, sealed with fire on the ninth autumn day, then practiced in solitude. At each station She is robed and fed, subtle as formula below, diagram-fine at the loins, coiled-powerful from the navel, honored with leaf at the heart, with lamps at the throat, with camphor and bridal flowers at the brow, then veiled with the Lord at the crown before returning to the base. Soul thus weds ground, and repetition makes the abode permanent. In practice, seek a qualified guide before forcing any ascent, and meanwhile rehearse the stations in gentle imagination. (Stotra verse 37.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-037"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-099", title: "Mūlādhāraikanilayā", category: "Kuṇḍalinī", summary: "She whose principal abode is the mūlādhāra. Root means origin and support means ground, so the base-wheel is the foundation-seat where She chiefly dwells, later chapters unfolding its full detail. Every ascent begins by honoring the ground floor. In practice, sit firmly today with attention dropped to the body's base, feeling support rise to meet you as Her welcome. Ground floors bear every upper story, and no tower of meditation stands long without this base honestly occupied, so beginners should love the foundation more than the dome, while adepts revisit it daily with gratitude, knowing all heights borrow their safety from this depth. (Stotra verse 38.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-038"] },
  { id: "ls-nama-100", title: "Brahmagranthivibhedinī", category: "Kuṇḍalinī", summary: "She who breaks through the knot of brahma. Three knots bar the coiled one's highway, and the first, tied above the base-wheel and below the sweetness-wheel, must be pierced before higher climbing begins. Each knot marks where identity clings, to body, to personality, to transcendence itself, and each piercing is a letting-go. In practice, loosen one body-clinging today, a vanity about looks or comfort, and feel the first knot slacken as attention rises freer. Knots in thread yield to patient fingers rather than to swords, and contractions of identity likewise loosen under steady compassionate attention instead of violent inner tearing. (Stotra verse 38.)", relatedVerseIds: ["ls-038"] },
];
